description:
Plan against docs/plans/2026-10-30-per-task-worktrees.md §2.2, §2.3, §2.7 and §2.9.

Objective: pin the landing-to-main behaviour in tests written BEFORE the implementation, so WT-08 and WT-09 must pass them unchanged. That removes any incentive to weaken them, and main's integrity is the epic's highest-stakes behaviour.

Deliverable: two new files, scripts/tests/worktree-landing.sh and its twin scripts/tests/worktree-landing.ps1. They drive the real wrapper post-flight in worktree mode against scratch repos, reusing the existing harness pieces (scripts/tests/postflight-fake-proxy.js and the way postflight.{sh,ps1} stage a project). The fake proxy answers claim/get_context with a `workspace` (WT-01). Each case is tagged `local` or `remote`, and `--only local|remote` and `--list` are supported.

Cases (at least these):
- local:
  - two tasks editing non-overlapping hunks of one file both land on main, each as one commit whose only parent is the previous main tip;
  - a true conflict lands nothing and main is unchanged;
  - main's reflog holds only the landing's update-ref entries, with linear first-parent history;
  - a local CAS where main already contains C counts as success;
  - a re-landing after a crash between push and sync moves nothing;
  - after a landing, moe/task/<id> fast-forwards to a commit containing C and W is clean;
  - the target checked out in another worktree gives MOE_LAND_TARGET_CHECKED_OUT;
  - a .moe path in the merged tree gives MOE_WORKTREE_BOARD_PATH.
- remote (bare origin with receive.denyNonFastForwards=true):
  - origin main gets C before local main moves, and no push uses a force flag;
  - a remote advanced by another clone triggers a retry that lands on top;
  - an unreachable remote prints MOE_PUSH_FAILED and local main still advances;
  - diverged local and remote targets give MOE_LAND_TARGET_DIVERGED, and nothing moves.
The files are NOT registered in CI (WT-09 does that) and must be red against current code.

NOT in scope: any change to scripts/moe-agent.{sh,ps1}; postflight.{sh,ps1}; CI wiring; gate and lease cases (WT-10 owns those); unlanded-route cases (WT-11).

Verification: bash -n scripts/tests/worktree-landing.sh && bash scripts/tests/worktree-landing.sh --list && ! bash scripts/tests/worktree-landing.sh && bash scripts/tests/parity-check.sh

implementationPlan.description:
The 8 local cases (no remote). Append them to CASES and add each `local <name>` line to the .ps1's $expectedCases.

Rules for every case:
- Assert git state first (refs, reflogs, parents, file bytes), then log codes or ledger rows.
- Include at least one expectation that only the worktree landing core can meet, so every case is red today.
- C is the landing's commit: the sha of the completion record_commit row, cross-checked against the ref.
- Hooks use the existing seam MOE_POSTFLIGHT_TEST_HOOK_PRE_UPDATE_REF. The worktree arm must run it once, after the receipt journal and before L9's publication (planningNotes, seams S1-S2).
- Every case uses one task, task-postflight. Peers are simulated by hooks or direct git, because the fake proxy hard-codes one task id.

1. `local hunks-merge`: non-overlapping hunks of one file both land, one commit each, each parented on the previous tip.
   - Setup: the CLI edits shared.txt line 9 in W. The hook runs peerCommit(P,'refs/heads/main', line 2 changed) = A, whose only parent is B0.
   - Expect:
     - main = C2 with parents exactly [A], and A's parents are [B0];
     - `git show main:shared.txt` has the peer's line 2, the task's line 9, and every other line unchanged;
     - no commit reachable from main has two parents;
     - exactly one completion row, outcome committed, sha C2, ref refs/heads/main;
     - a finalize `landed` with landedRevision C2.
2. `local conflict`:
   - Setup: the CLI edits line 9. The hook commits A on main, changing line 9 differently.
   - Expect:
     - main = A, so nothing landed;
     - no commit reachable from main carries the task's line 9;
     - the log has MOE_COMMIT_FAILED_CONFLICT;
     - no completion row with outcome committed, and no finalize landed;
     - the task's bytes survive: refs/heads/moe/task/task-postflight or a refs/moe/rescue/task-postflight/* ref holds the task's line 9.
   - Do NOT assert the finalize `unlanded` call; WT-11 adds it (WT-S comment 2).
3. `local reflog-linear`:
   - Setup: a plain landing (the CLI edits line 9).
   - Expect: `git log -g --format=%H refs/heads/main` lists exactly [C, B0]; C's parents are [B0]; `rev-list --count main` equals `rev-list --first-parent --count main`.
4. `local cas-contains-c`:
   - The hook reads journal(P): C = landedRevision, B = targetBefore. It asserts both are 40-hex. A missing journal is a FAIL, because the arm must journal before publishing (§2.2 L8, gate or not).
   - The hook then runs update-ref main C B, makes D = commit-tree C^{tree} -p C, and runs update-ref main D C. The wrapper's CAS from B then loses to a main that contains C.
   - Expect:
     - main = D, D^ = C, and C's parents are [B0];
     - a completion row committed with sha C, and a finalize landed with C;
     - the log lacks MOE_COMMIT_FAILED_REF_CONTENTION;
     - no refs/moe/rescue/task-postflight/* ref.
5. `local relanding-idempotent`:
   - Run 1: a plain landing, producing C.
   - Simulate a crash before the task-branch sync: update-ref refs/heads/moe/task/task-postflight to its tip's first parent (the pre-sync T), with the old value.
   - Run 2: the same seat; the CLI writes nothing.
   - Expect:
     - main is still C, and `rev-list --count main` is unchanged;
     - run 2's completion row has outcome `nothing` and code MOE_COMMIT_NOTHING_TO_COMMIT (§2.9);
     - main gains no second commit.
6. `local branch-sync`:
   - Setup: a plain landing. It may share case 3's run through a memoized fixture; no other case may share one.
   - Expect:
     - C is an ancestor of refs/heads/moe/task/task-postflight, and the tip's tree equals C's tree;
     - every entry of that branch's reflog is a fast-forward (old is an ancestor of new);
     - `git -C W status --porcelain --untracked-files=all` is empty, and W's HEAD is the task branch.
7. `local target-checked-out`:
   - Setup: `git -C P worktree add -q <caseDir>/main-wt main` before the run.
   - Expect:
     - the log has MOE_LAND_TARGET_CHECKED_OUT;
     - main = B0, and main-wt's shared.txt is unchanged;
     - no completion row committed, and no finalize landed.
8. `local board-path`:
   - Setup: the CLI edits line 9. Through opts.during it also commits a change to .moe/tasks/task-postflight.json onto refs/heads/moe/task/task-postflight with plumbing: a temp index read from the branch tip, because W is sparse without .moe/. This simulates the bug the L5b guard exists for.
   - Expect:
     - main = B0;
     - the log has MOE_WORKTREE_BOARD_PATH;
     - a refs/moe/rescue/task-postflight/* ref exists;
     - no completion row committed, and no finalize landed.

The git shim and the 7 remote cases. Each case uses a bare origin with receive.denyNonFastForwards=true, puts the shim first on PATH (remote cases only), and sets MOE_LAND_NET_BACKOFF_SEC=0 (§2.2 L4 / §2.6) to keep the runs short. Add each `remote <name>` line to the .ps1's $expectedCases.

Shim. The suite generates it into <caseDir>/shim at runtime, so each shell's version lives once, in the suite.
- Form:
  - sh: an executable bash script named `git`.
  - ps1: a script named `git.ps1`. Windows PowerShell 5.1 resolves a PATH-first git.ps1 ahead of git.exe and passes args such as `HEAD^{tree}` and `--format=%H %(describe)` intact. A git.cmd shim mangles `^`. Verified on this box, 2026-10-30.
- Env:
  - MOE_TEST_REAL_GIT: the absolute real git, resolved before the shim dir is prepended.
  - MOE_TEST_GIT_LOG: one appended line per call (subcommand plus argv), plus a FAILED or APPLIED-FAILED line for an intercepted call.
  - MOE_TEST_GIT_FAIL_FILE: a comma list, re-read on every call, so a CLI snippet can switch failures on mid-session. An empty or missing file means pass-through.
  - MOE_TEST_GIT_STATE: a dir; any push creates `pushed` in it.
- The subcommand is the first argument that does not start with '-', after skipping `-C <x>` and `-c <x>` pairs. Keep that global-option prefix for the shim's own git calls.
- Rules:
  - `fetch` → a stderr line, exit 128.
  - `fetch-after-push` (once `pushed` exists) → the same.
  - `push` → no output, exit 128, nothing applied.
  - `push-after-apply`:
    - snapshot refs/remotes/* (for-each-ref);
    - run the real push with stdout and stderr discarded;
    - restore the snapshot, because a real successful push moves the remote-tracking ref, and the simulated client never saw success;
    - exit 128.
  - Anything else → the real git with the same args and exit code: sh `exec`, ps1 `& $real @args; exit $LASTEXITCODE`.
- Self-check before the first remote case: run `git fetch nowhere` through the shim the way each wrapper resolves git (sh: bash with the shim PATH; ps1: powershell -NoProfile -Command with the shim PATH), with the fail set to `fetch`. Require exit 128 and a logged FAILED line. Otherwise every remote case is an ERROR naming the shim (exit 2), never a misleading FAIL.

Cases. C is as in step 2; the numbers in brackets are the WT-S comment's case numbers.
1. `remote push-first-no-force`:
   - Setup: no failures; the shim only logs.
   - Expect:
     - origin main = C, P main = C, and C's parents are [B0];
     - a completion row committed with sha C and pushed true; a finalize landed with C;
     - in the shim log, the first push line precedes the first update-ref line that sets refs/heads/main to C;
     - P main's reflog has exactly one new entry, B0→C;
     - no push argv contains --force, -f, --force-with-lease, --force-if-includes or --mirror, or a refspec starting with '+'.
2. `remote race-fetch-fails` (QA's two-seat race, [1]):
   - Setup: after W was cut from B0, an opts.during snippet plays seat A:
     - peerPush C1 (line 2);
     - in P, `fetch -q origin` and `update-ref refs/heads/main C1 B0` (A's publication plus its mirror);
     - write `fetch` into the fail file.
     The CLI also edits line 9 in W.
   - Expect:
     - origin main = C1, P main = C1, and refs/remotes/origin/main = C1;
     - the log has MOE_LAND_FETCH_FAILED and no MOE_LAND_TARGET_DIVERGED;
     - at least 3 FAILED fetch lines after the switch, and no push line;
     - no row with pushed true, and no finalize landed;
     - the task branch still holds line 9.
   - A is not a real seat, so the comment's "A finalizes landed" is out of reach. Say so in the case's comment.
3. `remote push-transport-fails` ([2]):
   - Setup: the fail set is `push` from the start.
   - Expect:
     - origin main = B0 and P main = B0;
     - the log has MOE_PUSH_FAILED and no MOE_PUSH_OUTCOME_UNKNOWN;
     - a FAILED push line followed later by a fetch line (the reconcile);
     - no row with pushed true, and no finalize landed.
4. `remote push-applied-client-fails` ([3]):
   - Setup: the fail set is `push-after-apply`.
   - Expect:
     - origin main = C with parents [B0], and P main = C;
     - a completion row committed with sha C and pushed true; a finalize landed with C;
     - neither MOE_PUSH_FAILED nor MOE_PUSH_OUTCOME_UNKNOWN in the log.
5. `remote push-outcome-unknown` ([4]):
   - Setup: the fail set is `push-after-apply,fetch-after-push`.
   - Phase 1 expect:
     - origin main = C (read from origin);
     - P main = B0 and refs/remotes/origin/main = B0, so no local ref moved;
     - the log has MOE_PUSH_OUTCOME_UNKNOWN, and a `MOE_RESCUE_REF ... sha=<C> reason=push-unknown` line whose ref resolves to C;
     - journal(P) exists, with pushResult starting `push unknown:`;
     - no completion row at all, and no finalize_attempt call.
   - Phase 2: empty the fail file and run the seat again with FAKE_CLAIM_MODE=idle. The pre-flight receipt replay runs right after the claim, idle or not.
   - Phase 2 expect:
     - P main = C;
     - journal(P) is gone;
     - a finalize landed with landedRevision C was recorded.
6. `remote push-rejected-retry` ([5]):
   - Setup: the hook runs peerPush P1 (line 2) before the first push, so that push is rejected.
   - Expect:
     - origin main = C2 with parents [P1], and P main = C2;
     - shared.txt has both edits;
     - a completion row committed with sha C2 and pushed true; a finalize landed with C2;
     - at least 2 push lines in the shim log;
     - origin's main reflog is exactly [C2, P1, B0], so the rejected C never landed.
7. `remote diverged` ([6]):
   - Setup: before the run, peerCommit P's main = X (local only), then peerPush P1.
   - Expect:
     - origin main = P1, and P main = X;
     - the log has MOE_LAND_TARGET_DIVERGED;
     - no push line, no row with pushed true, and no finalize landed.

Adversarial self-review of the two files, as the final step. Load the adversarial-self-review skill. Check each item:

- Would a correct WT-08/WT-09 pass every case? Re-read §2.2/§2.3/§2.6/§2.7/§2.9 and this row's two WT-S comments against each expectation. Remove any assertion that pins something those texts leave undecided: log wording beyond the codes, reflog or commit messages, helper names, round counts beyond the stated bounds.
- Would a wrong implementation fail? For each case, name the defect it catches:
  - a force push;
  - a local CAS fallback after a transport error;
  - a merge commit or a rewritten history on main;
  - landing over a conflict;
  - moving local main before the push;
  - publishing a .moe change;
  - landing onto a checked-out main;
  - a non-fast-forward task branch;
  - claiming `pushed` without remote proof.
- Determinism: no sleeps or timing races. Hooks fire once, the fail file is the only switch, each case owns its dirs, and nothing leaks into the developer's HOME, the shared %TEMP% or the real repository.
- Error handling: harness faults are ERROR (exit 2) and expectations are FAIL (exit 1). A timeout names its case. Temp dirs are removed on every exit path (the trap, and finally with a prefix check).
- Cross-platform: path.join throughout; quoted paths that contain spaces; .cmd and bash CLI launchers; the git.ps1 shim; LF endings; the .ps1's BOM.
- Size: count `git diff --numstat` additions for the two files (as untracked files, use `git add -N` on a throwaway index or `wc -l`). Compare them with the cap in this task's size-exception rail (proposal prop-72430aa5). If the rail is not approved yet, state the measured size in the completion summary.

If anything changes, re-run step 4's verification command and use that fresh output for complete_task.

comments.content:
Contract correction from the WT-S revision after QA reject 1. Where this comment and the row's description differ, the revised docs/plans/2026-10-30-per-task-worktrees.md and this comment win. Plan against revised §2.2, §2.3, §2.5, §2.6, §2.7 and §2.9.

WITHDRAWN: the case "an unreachable remote prints MOE_PUSH_FAILED and local main still advances". In remote mode a non-forced push is the only publication, and the local target moves only to commits the remote is known to hold.

Drive the remote cases with a deterministic git shim placed first on PATH. It fails or fakes chosen subcommands (for example MOE_TEST_GIT_FAIL=fetch|push|push-after-apply) and execs the real git for everything else.

Required `remote` cases:
1. QA's two-seat race. Seat A publishes C1, then all 3 of seat B's fetches fail. Expect:
   - B publishes nothing and ends unlanded `remote` (MOE_LAND_FETCH_FAILED);
   - local target == origin target == C1;
   - no MOE_LAND_TARGET_DIVERGED;
   - A finalizes `landed`.
2. Push transport error, then the reconcile fetch succeeds without C. Expect MOE_PUSH_FAILED, the round ends, the local target is unmoved, and nothing sends `pushed: true`.
3. Push applied but the client fails: the shim runs the real push, then exits 128 with no porcelain line. The reconcile finds C. Expect landed, local mirrored to C, `pushed: true`.
4. Unknown outcome: the push and the reconcile fetch both fail. Expect:
   - MOE_PUSH_OUTCOME_UNKNOWN, and no local ref moved;
   - the receipt journal kept, with push result `push unknown: …`;
   - a `push-unknown` rescue ref on C;
   - no completion ledger row and no finalize, so the attempt stays `finalizing`.
   Then, with the shim off, receipt_replay fetches, finds C and closes `landed`.
5. Rejected push: a peer landed first, so the line carries `!`. Expect a new round on the fresh tip, which lands.
6. The local target is not an ancestor of the fetched tip. Expect unlanded `diverged`, and nothing moves.

Keep the other listed cases. With no remote, L9's local CAS is the publication and `pushed` is false. The files must still be red against current code and stay out of CI.
